          "path": "_m/d/n/v/f/west-virginia-state-museum-wp/23-10-112-museum.jpg",
          "filename": "23-10-112-museum.jpg",
          "caption": "Scott Brothers Drug Store \n\n\n\nThe soda shop was a popular fixture in towns of the 1950s. These lunch counters, which specialized in sundaes, milkshakes, and phosphates, typically were located in drug stores, five-and-dimes, and department stores. \n\n\nThe Scotts operated a Charleston pharmacy and soda fountain from 1886 until the 1950s. In the 1890s, they built a turreted building that still stands on the corner of Capitol Street and Brawley Walkway. Some 20 years later, the Scotts moved their drug store here from another location on the same street. The Scott Brothers Drug Store was more elaborate than most other soda shops, featuring mahogany woodwork, terrazzo floors, sparkling marble work, and elegant chandeliers. The store also featured distinct globe lights, an original of which is on display in this room. \n\n\nThe table, stool, and mirrored panels are also from the Scott Brothers Drug Store in Charleston.",
          "alt": "Scott Brothers Drug Store \n\n\n\nThe soda shop was a popular fixture in towns of the 1950s. These lunch counters, which specialized in sundaes, milkshakes, and phosphates, typically were located in drug stores, five-and-dimes, and department stores. \n\n\nThe Scotts operated a Charleston pharmacy and soda fountain from 1886 until the 1950s. In the 1890s, they built a turreted building that still stands on the corner of Capitol Street and Brawley Walkway. Some 20 years later, the Scotts moved their drug store here from another location on the same street. The Scott Brothers Drug Store was more elaborate than most other soda shops, featuring mahogany woodwork, terrazzo floors, sparkling marble work, and elegant chandeliers. The store also featured distinct globe lights, an original of which is on display in this room. \n\n\nThe table, stool, and mirrored panels are also from the Scott Brothers Drug Store in Charleston.",
          "width": 2100,
          "height": 1386,
          "mimeType": "image/jpeg",
          "license": "CC BY 4.0",
          "artist": "Dsdugan",
          "sourceUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:23-10-112-museum.jpg",
          "sourceType": "commons-category",
          "sourceArticleId": "west-virginia-state-museum-wp",
